# Settings for docker compose (copy to .env, which git ignores). Logins and passwords are NOT
# here: each band's are in the database, sealed with the key in ./secrets (docker/make-secrets.sh).

# --- Bandzone (off / http; no browser is involved either way) ---
GIGSYNCHUB_BANDZONE_CLIENT=off
GIGSYNCHUB_BANDZONE_BANDSLUG=

# --- Bandsintown (off / http; the band signs in once in its OWN browser and pastes the result
#     into Settings — this server keeps only what that left, no password, no authenticator secret) ---
GIGSYNCHUB_BANDSINTOWN_CLIENT=off
GIGSYNCHUB_BANDSINTOWN_ARTISTNAME=
# GIGSYNCHUB_BANDSINTOWN_ARTISTID=
# GIGSYNCHUB_BANDSINTOWN_NOTIFYFOLLOWERS=false

# --- The address the app is reached at (Caddy; https://localhost when not set). Online, its DNS
#     must point to this server, ports 80 and 443 open; Caddy gets the certificate itself. The
#     sign-in (Rauthy) lives under https://$GSH_DOMAIN/auth/. Set it BEFORE the first start: the
#     app's client in Rauthy is made for it (docker/make-secrets.sh) — later, change the client's
#     addresses in Rauthy's admin (https://$GSH_DOMAIN/auth/v1/admin). ---
# GSH_DOMAIN=gigs.example.com

# --- The sign-in (Rauthy): its first admin (the password is in its log on the first start,
#     docker compose logs rauthy). Members of the Rauthy group "gigsynchub" may sign in; each
#     sees the bands they are a member of. ---
# GSH_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@localhost
# Rauthy's admin pages ask that admin for a passkey. A machine with nothing to make one with (no
# fingerprint, no phone to hand, no security key) can drop that for the password alone — on a
# server reachable from the internet, never: it is the sign-in's own admin.
# GSH_ADMIN_MFA=false
# Who owns the first band (the one the app ran for before it knew bands) — the first to sign in
# with this e-mail; not set = whoever signs in first.
# GIGSYNCHUB_ACCOUNTS_FIRSTOWNEREMAIL=you@example.com

# --- This machine ---
# GSH_APP_BIND=127.0.0.1        # the app's port 8080: this machine only; 0.0.0.0 = the local network too
# GSH_SUBNET=10.201.0.0/24      # the containers' network (pick one no VPN route uses)
# GSH_DNS=1.1.1.1               # the app's name server for the internet (platforms, calendar, towns)
# GSH_UID=1000                  # the user owning ./data and ./secrets (id -u / id -g)
# GSH_GID=1000

# --- What each container may take. The defaults suit one band's server; a machine with more
#     bands on it wants the app and the database larger, and a smaller one wants them smaller.
#     The app's heap follows its limit (-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75), so GSH_APP_MEM is the number
#     that decides it. Every container's log is capped at 3 × 10 MB whatever these say. ---
# GSH_DB_MEM=2g
# GSH_DB_CPUS=2
# GSH_APP_MEM=1g
# GSH_APP_CPUS=2
# GSH_AUTH_MEM=512m
# GSH_AUTH_CPUS=1
# GSH_PROXY_MEM=256m
# GSH_BACKUP_MEM=256m

# --- Where this server's traffic to a platform comes FROM. Unset = this machine's own address,
#     which is right for most people. Set it when the band's account has only ever been used from
#     one country and this server is somewhere else: ONE address you control (your own VPS, a paid
#     static one, an address in the band's country) — not a rotating list. These are the band's own
#     long-lived sessions, and a session arriving from a different address every time is the shape
#     of a stolen one, which is what account-takeover detection is built to catch. The proxy is
#     also, by construction, in the middle of that session: use one you would trust with it.
#     HTTP proxies only (java.net.http speaks no SOCKS); the password belongs in ./secrets. ---
# GIGSYNCHUB_BANDZONE_PROXY_HOST=
# GIGSYNCHUB_BANDZONE_PROXY_PORT=8080
# GIGSYNCHUB_BANDZONE_PROXY_USERNAME=
# GIGSYNCHUB_BANDSINTOWN_PROXY_HOST=
# GIGSYNCHUB_BANDSINTOWN_PROXY_PORT=8080
# GIGSYNCHUB_BANDSINTOWN_PROXY_USERNAME=

# --- Watching it (docker compose --profile observability up -d): Prometheus, Loki, Tempo and
#     Grafana, about 900 MB all told, Grafana on http://127.0.0.1:3000 of this machine only.
#     Set GSH_OTLP_ENABLED=true when you run it — the app then SENDS its traces to Tempo; left
#     false the spans are still made and their ids are still in every log line (which is what
#     `docker compose logs app | grep <traceId>` needs, and needs no stack at all). ---
# GSH_OTLP_ENABLED=true
# GSH_GRAFANA_PASSWORD=                 the admin's (default: admin; it is on 127.0.0.1 only)
# GSH_TRACE_SAMPLING=1.0                1.0 = every request; lower it only if the volume hurts
# GSH_SLOW_QUERY_MS=500                 a statement slower than this names itself in the log
# GSH_PROMETHEUS_MEM=512m
# GSH_LOKI_MEM=512m
# GSH_TEMPO_MEM=512m
# GSH_ALLOY_MEM=256m
# GSH_GRAFANA_MEM=512m

# --- One-time move from the H2 database: its SQL backup, copied into an EMPTY database on
#     start (the path inside the container; ./data is /app/data). Remove after the move. ---
# GIGSYNCHUB_DB_IMPORTH2=/app/data/backups/gigsynchub-h2-final.sql
